WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.530
CUNNINGHAM: Well, good morning.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:02.530
I'm Nelson Cunningham.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:03.530
I'm the president of McLarty Associates.

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:04.530
And I'll be presiding here at this morning's
session.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.530
Unlike most Council sessions, this one is
on the record.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.530
You'll see the cameras here.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:07.530
There's some reporters in the room.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.530
We do ask you to silence-to turn off your
mobile phones, because it interferes with

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:09.530
the sound system.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:10.530
We'll be-I'll introduce the vice president,
we'll spend-she and I-she'll make some remarks,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.530
she and I will spend until about 9:00 in a
dialogue, and then we'll open it to the audience,

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.530
as it typical here at the Council.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.530
For me, it's a privilege, of course, to preside
at any CFR event, but this one is a special

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.530
one for me, because my family lived in Panama
for six years when I was a boy, my father

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.530
was a businessman in Panama City.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:16.530
He founded the American Chamber there almost
40 years ago.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.530
And for us, Panama has a very special place
in my family's life.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.530
And I'm fortunate today, and for the last
20 years, to be involved in U.S.-Latin American

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:19.530
relations.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:20.530
And Panama, of course, has played such an
important role at key moments there.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:21.530
So it's a pleasure for me to be able to be
here to introduce the vice president.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:22.530
Vice President Saint Malo has a very international
background.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.530
She, of course, was raised in Panama, studied
in the United States for both college and

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:24.530
for her business degree, and then spent 15
years in Panama working for the U.N. Development

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:25.530
Program as the country manager there.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:26.530
In that role, she spent a great deal of time
both in Panama, and then dealing around the

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.530
world with multilateral issues.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.530
And I understand that she got to know the
current president, President Varela, during

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:29.530
that time, working with him on various dialogues
dealing with the canal and other issues.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:30.530
And so someone who came out of the multilateral
world, out of the business world, found herself

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.530
pulled into the political world.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:32.530
So you bring-you bring a great deal of experience
and breadth to this position.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:33.530
As is common in Panama-although you are of
course the constitutional vice president-but

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:34.530
as is common in Panama, the president also
asked you to take a second role as foreign

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.530
minister.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.530
And you now carry out that role.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.530
So you're here today in two capacities.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.530
It's a real pleasure to be able to introduce
to all of you Vice President Isabel de Saint

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:39.530
Malo.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.530
(Applause.)

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.530
SAINT MALO: Thank you very much, Nelson, for
those kind words.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:42.530
I was walking in and he greeted me in Spanish.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:43.530
And I said, wow, where is that Spanish from?

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.530
(Laughs.)

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:45.530
And he said: I've lived in Panama.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:46.530
So it's a pleasure to be sharing this podium
with you.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.530
Good morning, everybody.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.530
It's really an honor to be here today.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.530
I've been actually an admirer of the Council's
work for a long time, first as a student of

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.530
international affairs, then when I began my
career actually here in Washington, D.C. with

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.530
the Center for Democracy, and later on as
a practitioner of development and of democracy,

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:52.530
and now of diplomacy.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.530
You definitely make great contributions to
policymaking in terms of foreign policy.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:54.530
And that's-I think that's very important.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.530
So it's an honor to be here today.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:56.530
I've been told that the objective is to have
a conversation.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.489
So I'll try to be brief in these remarks so
that we move onto the interesting part of

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:01.890
the morning, which is the conversation with
you.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:07.430
I'll try to make some remarks to let you know
a little bit more about my country where we

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.560
are right now.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:16.340
And I'd like to begin by referring to Panama
as a global place.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:21.160
And I like to say, Panama has been globalized
before globalization was in fashion.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.340
And that's actually true.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.630
Ever since we were-we were born as a republic,
we've been-we've been a crossroads of people,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:28.630
of trade.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:34.299
And with the Porto Bello (sp) affairs, and
then through the gold rush, and then, of course,

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:35.299
the Panama Canal.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:40.560
And that has really made us who we are as
a country, a country really open to the world.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.270
And this has been kind of like the basis of
one of the principles of our foreign policy

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:53.479
and our domestic policy in terms of our ability
of bringing people together.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.710
Latin America enjoys today democracy, pretty
much.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:04.170
And not too long ago I'm sure around these
tables discussion of the dictatorships that

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:10.200
were taking place in the region were happening
every day.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.730
The guerilla warfare, we don't have that now.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:17.000
Fortunately, Panama has been part of that
democratization of the region.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.660
We are proud to have been-recuperated our
democracy.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.920
And for the past 25 years, we've had already
five democratically elected governments.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.430
And we enjoy democracy.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.930
Panama, our country, our people, we value
democracy.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.430
We value freedom of expression.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:42.660
We value a market economy, providing opportunities
to all of those that wish to seize those opportunities.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.170
In terms of our economy, we have a very diversified
economy.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:53.260
According to the IMF, we are ranked third
as the most economically prosperous country

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.840
in Latin America.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.950
In terms of the World Economic Forum we rank
second in terms of competitiveness.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:07.370
In the past 10 years, we've enjoyed a growth
of 7.6 percent, making it one of the fastest-growing

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.220
economies in Latin America.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:15.480
Even during the difficult years, recently,
Panama managed to continue to grow.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.909
We grew over 6 percent last year.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:23.620
Our projects for this year are also over 6
percent.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.569
And of course, the Panama Canal, which we
are very proud to be inaugurating this year

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:32.610
the enlarged Panama Canal, makes and important
part of our economy.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:39.930
However, our economy is very diversified and
we do not depend on any particular sector

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.819
of the economy.

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:47.849
Our canal is complemented by a worldwide platform
of logistics and services.

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:54.739
One of the best ports and airports in the
region are in Panama.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:04:01.270
And with the expanded canal, which we are
proud to be inaugurating June 26th, capacity

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:06.140
will triple in terms of volumetric capacity
to transit through the canal.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:14.180
And I was actually yesterday at the-at the
Energy Summit chaired by Vice President Biden.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:19.310
And we were discussing what this canal will
offer in terms of the possibility of transit

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:24.639
to LNG vessels from the United States Gulf
Coast to Asia.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:30.210
So all of this, and given the difficulties
of energy in the region and the efforts for

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:35.830
integration, there are great opportunities
and also possibilities for Panama to once

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.530
again put our country to the service of the
world, to the service of trade, and of development

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.280
in the region.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:51.919
Amidst this growth in economic terms, amidst
our democracy, which we are proud to have

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:55.650
recuperated, you would ask where are our challenges?

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:59.460
And we do have important challenges in terms
of development, particularly.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:09.000
Our challenges remain in terms of overcoming
poverty, in terms of improving social indicators.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.400
And that is actually the core of our government
plan.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.400
Our five-year government plan is concentrated
in providing these opportunities to almost

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:19.620
4 million Panamanians.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:30.810
We have development budget of over $17 million,
devoted primarily to education, to health.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:35.050
We've come a long way in terms of our poverty
indicators-a long way.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.810
But we still have important steps to take.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:43.210
And that is our concentration of our-of our
government.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.070
A country that grows, a country that has the
economic indicators that we have, a country

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:58.770
that serves the world has all of the necessary
capabilities to make this available to all

00:05:58.770 --> 00:05:59.770
Panamanians.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.460
And that is what we're working at.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.820
That is our concentration.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:10.990
And these investments in social and infrastructure
projects will definitely help us deepen our

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:18.419
social welfare, increase stability, expand
human capital, and further develop our infrastructure.

00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:23.180
In terms of our foreign policy, we base our
foreign policy on two pillars.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:28.849
The first pillar being a country that promotes
dialogue, that promotes understanding.

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:32.440
This has been part of our foreign policy for
many years.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:38.400
It's kind of who we are as a country, as a
result of having been the crossroads of the

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.759
Americas and of the world for a long time.

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:50.110
An example of this vision was the Summit of
the Americas, which we were proud to host

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.419
last year, and which allowed for the participation
of all countries from the Americas for the

00:06:54.419 --> 00:06:57.800
first time ever in the Summit of the Americas.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:03.630
I was that the-our effort was part of that
vision of Panama as a country that promotes

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.410
dialogue, that promotes understanding.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:10.880
Our second pillar in terms of foreign policy
is to have an active participation in the

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.319
global development agenda.

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:18.221
And we have been very, very active in this
government in terms of, for example, of human

00:07:18.221 --> 00:07:23.180
rights, which led to have a seat for the first
time.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:29.940
We were able to have a Panamanian elected
to the Inter-American Commission of Human

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.180
Rights for the first time ever.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:39.240
We were elected at the United Nations Commission
on Human Rights, also for the first time.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:45.099
And our participation in the Sustainable Development
Goals, which were agreed upon by many countries

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:52.570
last year at the United Nations General Assembly,
it's not only a commitment in terms of the

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:57.949
global development agenda, but it's at the
core of our national government plan.

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:06.840
We have actually decided by decree that the
SDGs will guide Panama's investments and Panama's

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.770
social efforts.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:15.069
So in this regard, there is a complete alignment
with the global development agenda and our

00:08:15.069 --> 00:08:21.099
domestic policy.

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:23.639
Our participation in the Paris agreement.

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:34.290
Panama even led an effort in terms of the
countries that have forest, and the efforts

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:39.170
that the world needs to make in order to ensure
that those countries that have forest can

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:43.750
keep them, and the contribution that this
makes to climate change.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:49.520
All of this is, again, a result of this importance
that we give to the global development agenda,

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.520
understanding that that is where the world
needs to concentrate its attention.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:04.090
We need, as a world, to overcome the issues
of today, the issues of people migrating from

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:09.590
countries where there is war and where there
is lack of opportunities, to developed countries.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:14.200
The issues of human rights, which still today
are violated in many parts of the world.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:19.530
The issues of incorporating those excluded
from development into development.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:29.650
We see these not as an issue that is apart
from our core domestic policy.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:36.590
And we see Panama as a country that can play
a role in order to continue to push those

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:37.980
efforts.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:48.410
Now, we are today in the mind frame of many
people not for everything that I've shared

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.410
with you.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:55.140
We are at the top of mind of many people because
of some publications that came out about a

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.500
month ago, already.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:03.190
It seems like a year ago-(laughter)-so much
has happened ever since-ever since that came

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:04.190
out.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.450
Let me tell you something about the wrongfully
called Panama Papers.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.990
And I'm sure they were named Panama Papers
because Panama is a great name.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.070
(Laughter.)

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.850
Panama is a great name.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:19.510
And this journalist needed to have a great
name for their publications.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:24.480
And, you know, that's our problem for having
a great name.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:32.580
But the fact of the matter is that, yes, the
publications referred to business conducted

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.080
by a law firm in Panama.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:42.730
But furthermore, the publications refer to
dealings of banks in 21 jurisdictions, none

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.600
of them in Panama.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:49.810
Not one of the banks that have been mentioned
in those publications is based in Panama.

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:52.710
The publications referred to offshores.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:55.400
Twenty percent of those, registered in Panama.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:11:05.000
Eighty percent of those, registered elsewhere-British
colonies, United States, everywhere.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:13.400
The publications really underline that there
is a global problem in terms of the financial

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:19.550
system, something that the world has recognized
for a long time and this publication just

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.650
make it clear that we still have a long way
to go.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:26.700
We still have a long way to go as a world.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.690
It's been hard on Panama that the publications
are named Panama Papers.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.540
It's been very hard, particularly for our
government.

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:46.910
I must admit that Panama, as a country, we
came a little late to the efforts of transparency

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:51.720
for the financial sector.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.310
When President Varela was vice president and
minister of foreign affairs, during the last

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:05.060
government, Panama had not signed one treaty
for double taxation.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:10.630
And under his leadership, we established a
national commission, we initiated our signing

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:13.240
of and negotiating of double taxation agreements.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.020
And we signed about 25 at that period of time.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:18.440
And then the alliance broke.

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:20.940
And then he left the government.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:26.270
And then the previous government didn't do
much in terms of this global effort.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:32.450
And when we took office two years ago, Panama
was on the gray list of the FATF because we

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:36.730
had failed to comply with some of the global
efforts.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:43.980
And we initiated a strategy which moved so
fast, even recognized so by the FATF.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.920
I heard them at a meeting in Panama say that
they had never seen a country move so fast

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:54.200
in terms of the decisions that need to be
made and the processes that need to be put

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:55.650
in place in terms of transparency.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:13:02.040
And we were able to be removed last year from
FATF's gray list as a result of Panama implementing

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:08.050
anti-money laundering legislation, prevention
of money from terrorism into our financial

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:09.050
services.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:12.130
We installed the governance institutions that
we needed to install.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:13.590
And we got our act together.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.780
And we were able to be removed from the gray
list.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:21.050
And we were able to move to the phase two
of the peer review of the OECD.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:25.500
So all of these efforts that our government
has been doing, and that have been recognized

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:31.210
globally because FATF's decision to remove
us from the list is a recognition of our efforts

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:37.620
and the OECD's transition to phase two of
the peer review is a recognition of our country's

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:43.640
efforts, it's hit hard by some publications
that have our name, because our name is pretty

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:44.640
nice.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:45.640
(Laughter.)

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:53.430
So we have been in an effort to, again, share
with the world what it is that we're doing.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:14:01.730
And, yes, there are problems with the financial
system around the world, problems of which

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:07.630
Panama must be a part of its solution, but
the rest of the world as well.

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:11.000
And we will continue to work in that direction.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.410
We are committed, not because of the Panama
Papers.

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.160
We were committed the day we took office.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:18.120
And the actions we've taken speak for themselves.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:23.190
But I wanted to take this opportunity to let
you know our vision on these publications

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:28.860
and what it is that our vision is in terms
of transparency in the financial sector, transparency

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:29.860
in general.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:36.620
Transparency has been at the core of our government
plan.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:40.080
Procurement in the past years in Panama has
been known.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:46.350
And actually, the justice system can say a
few things in that regard.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:54.700
Procurement in Panama was really permeated
by corrupt practices.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:15:00.740
We have really, really cleaned Panama's act
in that regard.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:08.220
And you can ask foreign companies now what
it is to invest in our government investment

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:09.220
plan.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.260
And we know what it was to invest previously.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:17.401
So our commitment to transparency in the financial
services, in our procurement processes, we

00:15:17.401 --> 00:15:24.370
are committed to changing politics in Panama
to really be what politics needs to be-a service

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.110
to the people.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:34.810
Politics-not to have a personal agenda, not
to enrich yourself, politics to be a tool

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:40.860
to serve the people and to serve the country.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:45.260
So our commitment is strong.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:52.610
Our commitment, once again, I would like to
reiterate, is not the result of responding

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:53.800
to some publications.

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:58.870
Our commitment has been clear ever since we
took office.

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:02.020
The measures taken speak for themselves.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:08.350
And as I began sharing with you, Panama is
a lot more than that.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:14.940
Panama is a country that enjoys democracy,
that enjoys freedom of expression, that enjoys

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:22.100
economic growth that is fighting to ensure
that this economic growth transform into the

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:26.540
improvement of life of all Panamanians.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:31.310
And we've seen some important progress in
that regard in terms of education, in terms

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:36.190
of health, in terms of water, and sanitation.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:41.430
And our success story is there.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:45.050
I invite you all to come see for yourself.

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:53.990
And more than that, we are truly committed
to continue to make Panama a country that

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:59.970
shares this prosperity with the rest of the
region, and that ensures that this prosperity

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:07.690
permeates not only Panama, but our neighbors
that have difficulties a lot harder than we

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:08.690
have.

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:18.260
And we are working hard in terms of our foreign
policy to play that role and make sure that

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:26.380
the global development agenda, which guides
multilateralism and foreign policy in many

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:32.770
ways, really reaches the people of Central
America, of Latin America.

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.270
And that's what we're committed to do.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:36.660
Thank you very much for your attention.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:38.360
(Applause.)

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:50.620
CUNNINGHAM: Madam Vice President, you touched
on one of the issues that I know has been

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:58.970
on the forefront of the minds of those here,
which is the transparency issues in Panama.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:05.760
Your boss, your president, President Varela,
as you mentioned, was vice president in the

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:12.120
prior administration, was foreign minister,
and had a very notorious split with President

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:19.160
Martinelli because Vice President Varela no
longer had confidence in the way President

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:24.180
Martinelli was governing the country when
it came to transparency and corruption issues.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:28.480
And he was, of course, relieved of his duties
as foreign minister, but he could not be relieved

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:30.070
of his duties as vice president.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:37.070
So he had an empty desk for a number of years,
which prepared him well to take on the role

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:41.490
of running-again, running for president, and
now serving as president.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:45.980
President Martinelli, meanwhile, former President
Martinelli is living in Miami.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:48.520
SAINT MALO: What can I tell you?

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:54.240
CUNNINGHAM: Your government has-your Supreme
Court has issued some charges against him.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.460
I know you have an extradition request pending.

00:18:56.460 --> 00:19:01.010
There may be further charges-if one reads
the newspapers-there may be further charges

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.010
coming.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:08.520
What can you tell us about the status of President
Martinelli here in the U.S. and your efforts

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.960
to bring him to justice in Panama?

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.860
SAINT MALO: Interesting question.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:22.660
As you have mentioned, our Supreme Court has
a few cases pending on Mr. Martinelli, former

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.300
President Martinelli.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:33.520
One of those-which is for the issue of rigging
telephone conversations-one of those has been

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:42.690
approved by the board of the Supreme Court
to, under that-under those charges request

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.510
the extradition to the United States.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:54.030
Now, in terms of Panamanian legal framework,
the Supreme Court needs to prepare the extradition

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:58.440
request and submit it to the Ministry of Foreign
Affairs.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:04.000
And then the Ministry of Foreign Affairs will
be responsible for transferring that request

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.700
to the United States.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:11.190
We have not yet received from the Supreme
Court that request for extradition.

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:15.990
Now, we've of course evaluated and studied
the issue.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:19.850
And it's a complicated situation.

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:25.220
We understand in terms of-first of all, our
extradition treaty with the United States

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:29.390
dates as far as the beginning of the 1900s.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:38.660
So the crime by which the Supreme Court is
authorized to request the extradition is not

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:42.610
part of the treaty because, of course, at
that time, there were no telephones, so even

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:47.890
less there was a crime to listen to conversations.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:52.370
Of course, this can all-and we've had discussions
with the United States authorities-of course,

00:20:52.370 --> 00:20:55.550
this can be supported.

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:58.690
But that's an issue that we need to deal with.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:05.780
The second issue is that according to the
treaty, if we request Mr. Martinelli's extradition

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:16.720
based on that case, we cannot judge him in
Panama for any other case.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:26.530
And he-the cases-I believe it's almost 10
cases that are in the Supreme Court regarding

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:28.290
Mr. Martinelli right now.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:36.200
And most of them are a lot more serious than
listening into conversations, which is serious

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:37.540
enough.

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:44.630
The charges are basically referring to corruption,
and his participation on corruption practices,

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:51.600
on our procurement processes, and even linked
to processes that are now at the judicial

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.520
system in the United States.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:59.880
So another issue that I understand our Supreme
Court has is, how are we going to request

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:09.970
his extradition and make sure, in the dealings
with the United States, that we are not just

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:16.690
protecting him from future processes?

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:24.530
So those issues are critical issues there,
and as of-as we have been told by our Supreme

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:35.190
Court, and actually we've discussed, as mentioned,
with U.S. authorities, the importance of making

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:41.120
a very strong case so that the case doesn't
fall through the cracks in the United States

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:42.230
system.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:48.650
And we do know that Mr. Martinelli has the
resources to hire knowledgeable lawyers, and

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:55.010
we understand he has hired a large number
of knowledgeable lawyers.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:58.330
That's an issue for Panama.

00:22:58.330 --> 00:23:02.780
We need to make sure that we can process him
properly for all of these issues.

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:05.220
Now, that's one thing, the request for extradition.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:09.810
And I do hope that we will request his extradition
soon enough.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:17.430
It's not in our hands, but we will definitely
go on with the process once we receive a request.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:18.430
That is one thing.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:25.290
But the second thing is what's happening in
the United States with the fact that former

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:31.222
President Martinelli is living in the United
States.

00:23:31.222 --> 00:23:40.860
Now, I understand that your authorities cannot
mention, for example, if he has requested

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:41.860
asylum.

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:44.130
He might have.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:50.380
Some of you might be lawyers and would probably
know better than I what happens in terms of

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:57.340
an extradition request if he's in an asylum
request process.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:03.250
And I understand asylum request processes
can take as long as three years for a decision.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:11.830
And I understand that once this is-this process
is going on, this person cannot be touched.

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:19.170
So, when and if Panama requests the extradition,
I don't know what's going to happen with the

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:22.320
United States system.

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:35.470
But what is-what is harder, I think, is not
this judicial situation that I do hope for

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:40.430
Panama's sake goes through.

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:48.930
But the thing is that this person is living
in the United States really just dedicated

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:53.230
to Panamanian politics through social media.

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:59.820
So I don't know, that is something that I'd
like to pose the question to you.

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:00.820
(Laughter.)

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.010
You know, it just blows my mind.

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.710
It blows my mind.

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:14.380
And I think-I think the world has a long way
to go to ensure that we stop protecting those

00:25:14.380 --> 00:25:18.280
that should be brought to justice.

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:22.880
CUNNINGHAM: I want to commend you, in your-in
your remarks and just now, for the candor

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:27.120
in which you've addressed some of the most
complicated issues facing Panama today-the

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:32.530
Panama Papers, the issues with former President
Martinelli, the issues of development.

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:38.809
You mentioned also-but I think you swallowed
some of the good news, which is of course

00:25:38.809 --> 00:25:43.710
the opening of the-of the new locks in the
Panama Canal.

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:48.460
Forty years ago here in the United States,
Panama was one of the hottest political topics

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:53.930
over President Carter's-over, actually, President
Ford and then President Carter's negotiation

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:59.350
of a treaty to return the Canal Zone to Panama
and to eventually turn over the management

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:02.130
of the Panama Canal to the Panamanians.

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:09.510
Those who may not have focused on Panama since
then might not know that Panama successfully

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:13.800
took over the management of the canal and
have had really an unimpeachable, for the

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:20.110
last 20 years-almost 20 years, management
of the canal, belying all of the fears and

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:22.920
concerns that had been raised at the time.

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:27.900
You've also gone ahead and then moved ahead
with a major expansion of the canal to permit

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:29.810
much larger vessels to go through.

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:32.900
Tell us how-and that will open in June, as
you mentioned.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:41.460
Tell us how you expect that to impact Panama's
economy, as well as further the global shipping

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:42.700
lanes that go through Panama?

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:47.900
SAINT MALO: Thank you for that question, because
we are very proud in Panama to be inaugurating

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:51.540
our expanded canal June 26th.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:59.200
And let me share with you what I think we're
most proud of.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:04.260
And you've mentioned that we've successfully
assumed the administration of the canal, and

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:12.360
that it's recognized internationally by the
trade world and by other countries the transition

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:18.260
from U.S. management to Panamanian management
was imperceptible to the world.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:20.220
That was-we've done it.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.480
We've done it well.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:28.580
But more than that, over a hundred years ago,
when we began the original canal, we had to

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:33.750
turn to the French and that we had to turn
to the United States, but this expansion was

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:35.360
done by Panama.

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:44.220
And if we look at the investment, which is
over 5,000 million dollars, in terms of dollars

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.890
in that time we're building a second canal.

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:55.450
This is a major infrastructure project, and
we are very proud to be inaugurating the canal,

00:27:55.450 --> 00:28:03.030
and being a Panamanian-a Panamanian project
funded by Panama, led by Panama, contracted

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:04.460
by Panama.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:09.780
And this just shows what Panama is capable
of.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:16.230
And this will represent a major transition
to world trade.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:23.580
I mentioned the LNG vessels, for example,
that will be able to transit through an enlarged

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:24.580
canal.

00:28:24.580 --> 00:28:29.360
The administrator of the canal was just saying
a few days ago that ever since the inauguration

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:41.040
was announced, they have just been receiving
the bookings, and it's booked for its initiation.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:47.600
The world-the maritime sector has been expecting
the expansion of the canal and were just there

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:52.930
ready to jump as soon as it was finalized,
and that's great news.

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:58.760
An enlarged canal will represent important
additional revenues for Panama.

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:05.010
One of the most important things from the
transfer-for the transfer of the canal to

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:14.370
Panamanian management is that, under U.S.
administration, the canal was managed-in terms

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:23.180
of its budget, it was managed-money that came
in, money that was-that came out.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:27.000
There were no revenues for Panama, nor the
United States.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:31.410
That was definitely changed when Panama assumed
administration, and the canal is today an

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:36.059
important source of income for our national
budget.

00:29:36.059 --> 00:29:40.540
And an expanded canal will increase that dramatically.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:49.860
And we are-we are waiting for those resources
in order to divert them to what I have mentioned

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:54.640
is our most important challenge, which is
development.

00:29:54.640 --> 00:30:00.320
As mentioned earlier, our challenges in terms
of water and sanitation are amazing.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:03.330
You go to Panama City-I don't know if you've
been recently; if you haven't been recently,

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:10.650
you should go-you go to Panama City, and Panama
City looks like New York City.

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:20.250
But in a country where we have a city that
is booming, we have sectors of our country

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.020
that are excluded.

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:30.251
And we're working hard at ensuring that our
human development indicators just jump, and

00:30:30.251 --> 00:30:34.990
the resources that we will receive from an
expanded canal will definitely be diverted

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:36.120
into that-into that direction.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:42.200
CUNNINGHAM: I'm going to turn now to the audience.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:48.540
Those of you who have questions, I'll ask
you first to tell us who you are, what institution

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:52.250
you're with, and then to keep your questions
brief, fierce, and decisive.

00:30:52.250 --> 00:31:00.500
Well, the press has an interest in asking
questions, so let me turn to the table here.

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:03.770
The woman in the-in the red jacket, please.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:05.380
Q: Hi.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:07.809
Welcome, Madam Secretary.

00:31:07.809 --> 00:31:11.140
My name is Nadia Chow, Washington correspondent
for Liberty Times.

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:12.510
I have two question(s).

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:19.190
The first one is, for the inauguration, Panama
already extended an invitation to Taiwanese

00:31:19.190 --> 00:31:23.790
President-elect Tsai Ing-wen and both Chinese
President Xi Jinping.

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:28.020
I wonder, are you expecting both of them to
attend?

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:34.040
Because Madam Tsai already expressed his (sic)
interest to, you know, participate.

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:37.190
The second question, the Panama Paper(s).

00:31:37.190 --> 00:31:42.730
More detail were coming on Monday, next Monday,
and you said that Panama has been hit hard

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:43.730
by this paper.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:49.410
Can you elaborate, you know, if more detail
is coming, what the impact will be on Panama's

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:50.410
economy?

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:51.410
Thank you.

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:52.950
SAINT MALO: Thank you very much.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:58.230
On the first question, yes, we have extended
invitations to both the president of China

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:02.580
Taiwan and the president of People's Republic
of China.

00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:10.330
Panama has diplomatic relations with Taiwan
and Panama has commercial relations with China.

00:32:10.330 --> 00:32:12.100
We have an office in China.

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:14.420
We have an office in Hong Kong.

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:16.519
And we have an office in Taiwan.

00:32:16.519 --> 00:32:24.000
And we have invited to the inauguration of
the Panama Canal the commercial sector of

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.100
the world, and we have invited heads of state.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.580
Now, what has defined which heads of state
we have invited?

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:32.170
We invited about 40 heads of state.

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:40.299
The decision has been based primarily on those
countries that are the primary users of the

00:32:40.299 --> 00:32:44.860
Panama Canal, and China is one of the primary
users of the Panama Canal.

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:46.770
So we would love to have them both, definitely.

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:57.430
This is-this is an event where Panama places
ourselves once again to the service of world

00:32:57.430 --> 00:33:04.730
trade and world commerce, and we hope to share
this celebration with the rest of the countries

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:09.550
that participate of this-of this service that
Panama provides.

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:15.900
Regarding the second question, yes, we've
heard that there is a second round of information

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:16.900
coming.

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:22.600
I said Panama has been hit hard because it
bears Panama's name.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:27.270
But many countries have been hit hard because
the information that has come out here touches,

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:35.470
as I mentioned, 21 jurisdictions in terms
of banking, many other jurisdictions in terms

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:46.360
of the registration of offshores, and furthermore,
people that have a public life all over the

00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:48.220
world.

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:51.530
What can come of those-of those publications?

00:33:51.530 --> 00:34:04.080
I would imagine more information on people
that have offshores or that have registered

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:06.160
businesses.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:13.590
And we're just as expecting as the rest of
the world of these new publications.

00:34:13.590 --> 00:34:14.909
CUNNINGHAM: Good.

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:18.690
Yes, here at the front table?

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:21.690
Q: Good morning.

00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:23.800
Thank you for joining us.

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.389
Clara Brillembourg of the law firm Foley Hoag.

00:34:26.389 --> 00:34:31.000
I wanted to ask, given Panama's historic role
as a leader in the region, and also given

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:35.919
your close relationship with the United States,
how the opening of the relationship between

00:34:35.919 --> 00:34:38.690
the United States and Cuba has affected Panama.

00:34:38.690 --> 00:34:44.460
SAINT MALO: The opening of the relationship
with the United States and Cuba, it's great

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:46.240
news to Latin America.

00:34:46.240 --> 00:34:49.600
It's not only great news to Panama, but it's
great news to Latin America.

00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:56.730
It was a-it was something that needed to happen
at some point.

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:03.820
We are very proud to have been the venue where
the president of the United States and the

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:08.090
president of Cuba met for the first time over
50 years.

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:13.640
And it's interesting that the last time they
had met before the Summit of the Americas,

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:20.350
the last time a president of the United States
and a president of Cuba had met, was in 1956

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:22.440
in Panama.

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:31.270
So we take pride in being a country that brings
people together, that brings countries together.

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:33.910
We have been open to the world forever.

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:42.260
Due to our geography, geographic location,
Panama has been a crossroads of people, of

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:43.640
culture, of trade.

00:35:43.640 --> 00:35:50.690
And that has made us open to the world, and
that has made us a country that takes pride

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:53.620
in bringing people and countries together.

00:35:53.620 --> 00:36:00.820
So that's great news, and we do hope it will
continue to move forward.

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:04.740
CUNNINGHAM: Let me ask a question that follows
on the one about the expansion of the Panama

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:11.180
Canal, because this-the opening of the canal
and the new locks actually touches on another

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:18.910
topic of global concern, because there may
not be enough water to fully power the locks

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:23.510
going forward, as I understand, at least for
the moment, because of El Nino.

00:36:23.510 --> 00:36:28.109
It's-not everyone may know that one of the
miracles of the Panama Canal is that it's

00:36:28.109 --> 00:36:31.980
fed entirely by the waters of the rain forest
in the interior of Panama.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:33.410
There are no pumps.

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:34.470
It's not recirculated.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:38.359
The water starts in the interior of Panama
and then flows through the locks into the

00:36:38.359 --> 00:36:39.550
sea.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.070
Because of El Nino, you've had a drought.

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:49.550
Some have linked El Nino, and this particularly
severe El Nino, to climate change.

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:55.480
What is Panama doing to both address the immediate
issue of the water that it needs for the locks

00:36:55.480 --> 00:36:58.590
and the broader question of climate change?

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:03.880
SAINT MALO: Well, I'll begin with the second
question first, the broader question of climate

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:04.880
change.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:08.670
As mentioned, we were very active in the negotiations
towards the Paris agreement.

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:13.250
We think that's great news to the world that
the world has finally reached an agreement

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:14.910
in terms of climate change.

00:37:14.910 --> 00:37:19.360
The implementation will definitely be difficult.

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:27.570
There is a question here of the cost of taking
measures and who bears the cost.

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:34.480
When I mentioned the efforts of Panama, which
led-Panama led a coalition of 52 countries

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:41.200
towards the negotiations of the Paris agreement,
52 countries that have forest.

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:50.400
And the proposal of these 52 countries was
basically what do we do to conserve this forest,

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.460
and who foots the bill?

00:37:52.460 --> 00:38:00.150
Because at the end there is a cost to development
when you commit to conserving your forests.

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:04.320
And forests are basically in the underdeveloped
world.

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:12.090
So countries like the United States will have
an important role to play in this effort towards

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:13.490
climate change.

00:38:13.490 --> 00:38:16.290
Locally, we are doing a lot of things.

00:38:16.290 --> 00:38:23.970
We have a strong leader of the efforts right
now in Panama, which is our minister of environment.

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:26.860
She's been an advocate of environment for
a long time.

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:32.370
She's been trained in environment, and she's
working in this regard.

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:39.320
We have a project of reforesting Panama, which
is going very well.

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:46.670
And we're just really turning around how we
manage environmental processes in Panama,

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:54.870
and we've really raised the commitment of
our government in terms of environment in

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:55.960
general.

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.330
Now, the water for the canal.

00:38:59.330 --> 00:39:06.781
The issue is that the lakes that feed the
canal are also the lake that feed the Panamanian

00:39:06.781 --> 00:39:09.660
population for drinking water.

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:12.400
So, yes, this is an important issue.

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:18.920
The good news is that the new canal does recirculate
water.

00:39:18.920 --> 00:39:26.460
So the locks that were built at the beginning
of the last century, they were throwing that

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:29.270
water into the sea.

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:33.109
But the new locks work recirculating water.

00:39:33.109 --> 00:39:35.910
So that's the good news.

00:39:35.910 --> 00:39:41.400
We have-President Varela mentioned last year
at his speech at the National Assembly a project

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:48.570
that we need to look into soon, which is a
project to build further water reserves in

00:39:48.570 --> 00:39:49.570
Panama.

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:57.021
And the lakes that feed the canal are manmade
lakes, are lakes that were made for the canal,

00:39:57.021 --> 00:40:01.180
and they now provide the drinking water to
the city.

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:03.650
There is a lot of water in Panama.

00:40:03.650 --> 00:40:06.460
We need to work at preserving this water.

00:40:06.460 --> 00:40:12.780
And there is an area of Rio Indio where there
are several rivers come together.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:20.370
And we're looking towards in the future just
building a large new water reservoir-reservoir.

00:40:20.370 --> 00:40:26.580
Now, the other good news is that in Panama
we had El Nino.

00:40:26.580 --> 00:40:29.930
But in Panama, when it starts raining, it
starts raining.

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:30.930
(Laughter.)

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:34.340
And last week it started raining, and then
we had floods.

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:35.340
(Laughs.)

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:40.359
So fortunately enough, the dry season has-it's
over.

00:40:40.359 --> 00:40:44.340
The lakes will-how do you say that?

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:47.410
They will regain-
CUNNINGHAM: Replenish.

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:52.760
SAINT MALO: Replenish-thank you-replenish
with water.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:59.210
But that's an issue that Panama needs to look
into for the future, the issue of water for

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:02.520
the canal and water for our population.

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:08.110
We're fine for the next-you know, for the
near future.

00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:15.560
But long term we're already looking into additional
projects that will ensure the supply of water.

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:19.760
CUNNINGHAM: Yes, the gentleman at the back
table.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:23.300
Q: Thank you.

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:24.730
Madam Secretary, this is Alex.

00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:28.480
I am the Washington correspondent of United
Daily News of Taiwan.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:34.520
I have a question with regard to diplomatic
tie between Panama and Taiwan.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:43.810
We know Panama and China have robust economic
ties, which China have diplomatic tie with

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:44.810
Taiwan.

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:53.170
And Panama has been expressed its willingness
to establish diplomatic tie with China before.

00:41:53.170 --> 00:42:03.340
And my question is, how would you describe
the diplomatic relations between Taiwan and

00:42:03.340 --> 00:42:05.460
Panama at present?

00:42:05.460 --> 00:42:16.170
And is so-called dual recognition a possible
way to figure out the diplomatic issue among

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:18.790
Panama, Taiwan, and China?

00:42:18.790 --> 00:42:20.090
Thank you.

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:25.460
SAINT MALO: We have diplomatic relations with
Taiwan.

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:30.080
We have very good diplomatic relations.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:34.150
We have, as I have mentioned, commercial relations
with China.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:43.560
We understand the two Chinas have an agreement,
currently a truce, which we respect.

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:56.000
And we are very respectful of the way they
handle their relations and their rapprochement.

00:42:56.000 --> 00:42:59.250
It's been a long process for them.

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:05.780
Meanwhile, we are friends of Taiwan and have
diplomatic relations with Taiwan.

00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:09.790
And we have strong relations, commercial relations,
with China.

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:17.050
And we do hope that both of them will continue
to become stronger.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:23.640
CUNNINGHAM: Yes, sir, over here.

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.280
Q: Hello.

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:27.280
Steve Rodriguez.

00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:32.070
I'm a venture capitalist, but I spent some
time in the region with the U.S. Mil Group

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:34.010
in Colombia.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:40.980
My question, Madam Vice President, is your
country sits at the crossroads of a number

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:48.520
of important issues related to terrorism,
epidemiology, and, of course, trade.

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:54.010
I guess my question is not so much getting
a current update of where things are.

00:43:54.010 --> 00:43:58.960
I can read plenty of papers that have been
written here in D.C. on that.

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:03.841
My question is, how do you view your country's
role at the crossroads of so many of those

00:44:03.841 --> 00:44:05.720
issues going forward?

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:17.070
SAINT MALO: Those issues that are global issues
are-we are having to face them, as well as

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:19.740
the rest of the world.

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:26.940
And I take the opportunity of your question
to refer to one particular issue, which is

00:44:26.940 --> 00:44:33.480
difficult for us and which is a current affair,
in the situation of migrants going through

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:37.810
the isthmus on their way to the United States.

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:47.170
For the past years, many years, there have
been-there has been a flow of migrants through

00:44:47.170 --> 00:44:50.270
the Central American isthmus to reach the
United States.

00:44:50.270 --> 00:45:00.480
Now, the flow is made up of Cubans, many of
them wanting to reach the United States, given

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:08.849
the policy that the United States has of welcoming
Cuban migrants.

00:45:08.849 --> 00:45:16.720
And there has also been a flow of people from
another continent.

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:22.710
There is a flow of people from Africa, from
Asia.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:30.340
These people enter the continent, most of
them, through Brazil or Ecuador.

00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:39.000
Brazil and Ecuador both have a migrant policy
that they're open to the world and they don't

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:43.869
require visas for many nationalities.

00:45:43.869 --> 00:45:45.520
And I think it's a drama.

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:53.150
I think it's a human drama to have people
that flee their countries with their families.

00:45:53.150 --> 00:45:56.630
We have people going with their young children.

00:45:56.630 --> 00:45:58.500
We have pregnant women.

00:45:58.500 --> 00:46:02.280
We have entire families.

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:09.910
And, you know, the issue of refugees in Europe,
it's very visible right now because they're

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:12.170
coming in large numbers.

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:18.390
The issue of migrants going through the Central
American isthmus towards the United States,

00:46:18.390 --> 00:46:23.180
it's a smaller number than the refugees in
Europe so it's not as known.

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:25.010
But it's a human drama.

00:46:25.010 --> 00:46:33.110
It's a human drama that people have situations
in their country in terms of wars, in terms

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:41.650
of lack of economic opportunities, that are
so dramatic that they're willing to risk everything

00:46:41.650 --> 00:46:45.520
to find a better livelihood.

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:51.100
And I really think the world needs to look
into what's happening in these countries.

00:46:51.100 --> 00:47:01.120
The response is not closing the gates, because
at some point I don't know if you can close

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:02.120
the gates.

00:47:02.120 --> 00:47:05.460
I mean, look what's happening in Europe.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:12.859
But I think the response needs to be a coherent,
integral response.

00:47:12.859 --> 00:47:18.160
Addressing the issues of development and the
issues of peace are countries that are far

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:25.130
away from us, but that have these issues.

00:47:25.130 --> 00:47:35.550
And the issue of migrants, right now we have
3,900 Cubans in Panama that cannot move to

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:38.450
Costa Rica because Costa Rica closed the frontiers.

00:47:38.450 --> 00:47:42.970
And Costa Rica closed the frontiers because
Nicaragua closed the frontiers.

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:47.250
And we cannot close the frontiers with Colombia
because we don't have a frontier with Colombia.

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:54.660
We have a jungle on our frontier with Colombia.

00:47:54.660 --> 00:47:56.240
So what are we to do?

00:47:56.240 --> 00:48:03.720
Are we to move these families with four-,
three-, two-year children into the jungle,

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:07.740
which we actually did with a few of them a
couple of weeks ago, and a four-year-old got

00:48:07.740 --> 00:48:08.740
lost?

00:48:08.740 --> 00:48:10.920
And then we sent our police.

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:16.580
After sending our police to stop them from
coming in, we sent our police to look for

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:21.140
the kid, because what are you going to do,
ignore this human drama?

00:48:21.140 --> 00:48:27.099
I mean, we cannot be a world talking about
a global agenda and talking about poverty

00:48:27.099 --> 00:48:34.300
and talking about human rights at the tables
of the U.N. and then looking to the other

00:48:34.300 --> 00:48:41.570
side when there are people that are willing
to risk everything because they have no future

00:48:41.570 --> 00:48:45.849
at their original countries.

00:48:45.849 --> 00:48:48.359
I don't see an easy response.

00:48:48.359 --> 00:48:52.550
Panama is going to have to do something at
some point.

00:48:52.550 --> 00:49:00.380
We're going to have to-I don't know-send Cubans
back to Cuba, send them to Ecuador, which

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:02.300
issued the visas.

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:03.630
They have Ecuadorian visas.

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:05.210
They don't have visas from Colombia.

00:49:05.210 --> 00:49:08.440
They don't have visas from Panama.

00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:16.970
We received about 400-a few months ago about
400, 390, people from Congo.

00:49:16.970 --> 00:49:18.230
And we were, like, what?

00:49:18.230 --> 00:49:20.940
Three hundred and ninety in one month?

00:49:20.940 --> 00:49:24.440
We've had people from Senegal, from Congo;
you know, two, three.

00:49:24.440 --> 00:49:28.920
They come through Brazil and they walk their
way up to the United States.

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:30.310
Can you believe this?

00:49:30.310 --> 00:49:34.720
So we were, like, this doesn't make sense,
you know, 390.

00:49:34.720 --> 00:49:42.740
Well, we have some agreements with the United
States and some systems to identify some people

00:49:42.740 --> 00:49:47.590
through their-what do you call this, their-
CUNNINGHAM: Fingerprints.

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:55.660
SAINT MALO: -fingerprints, thank you, because
they claim they don't have papers.

00:49:55.660 --> 00:49:59.310
They "lose" their papers because they don't
want to be sent back.

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:03.510
And if countries don't know where they're
from, you cannot send them back.

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:07.290
They were Haitians.

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:11.130
They were Haitians claiming to be from Congo.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:15.680
So this is a human drama that the world needs
to start looking at.

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:18.110
And I don't think the result is to close the
gates.

00:50:18.110 --> 00:50:21.700
I think the result is to push development
in those countries.

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:27.970
And I don't think we can any longer pretend
that this does not affect us, because we thrive

00:50:27.970 --> 00:50:33.760
in development and economic prosperity because
this is not so far away when these people

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:37.250
are every day coming into our own countries.

00:50:37.250 --> 00:50:42.190
CUNNINGHAM: Well, thank you for that very
articulate description of the issues involved.

00:50:42.190 --> 00:50:47.200
Our country is about to embark on a-on a debate
on this very issue, a presidential debate.

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:51.280
And it's one of those who believes that America
is much stronger when it welcomes those from

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:57.560
around the world, I personally welcome your
articulate comment, although it was not intended

00:50:57.560 --> 00:51:01.700
to be a comment at all affecting our own political
debate here.

00:51:01.700 --> 00:51:02.700
Other questions?

00:51:02.700 --> 00:51:03.850
Yes, sir, in the back.

00:51:03.850 --> 00:51:05.900
Q: Hi, my name is Tony Perez.

00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:07.760
I'm a-
CUNNINGHAM: Wait for the microphone, sir.

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:08.760
Q: Yeah, thank you.

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:10.020
My name is Tony Perez.

00:51:10.020 --> 00:51:11.530
I'm a professor at Catholic University.

00:51:11.530 --> 00:51:16.060
And earlier in my career, I had the lack of
fortune of working on international extradition

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:20.080
matters, so I was intrigued by your comment
about the Martinelli situation.

00:51:20.080 --> 00:51:24.220
The United States for the last 30, 40 years
to my knowledge has been seeking to renegotiate

00:51:24.220 --> 00:51:28.950
extradition treaties into the modern dual-criminality
format.

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:34.360
And I was wondering-if I understood you correctly,
Panama has not yet joined in that process,

00:51:34.360 --> 00:51:37.849
and I was wondering if you're changing your
view about it, and whether or not you could

00:51:37.849 --> 00:51:40.770
utilize that change to extradite Mr. Martinelli?

00:51:40.770 --> 00:51:45.580
And the follow-up question is: Are you really
better off with Martinelli in Panama or in

00:51:45.580 --> 00:51:46.580
Miami?

00:51:46.580 --> 00:51:47.580
(Laughter.)

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:50.290
SAINT MALO: I don't know if I want to answer
your second question.

00:51:50.290 --> 00:51:51.290
(Laughter.)

00:51:51.290 --> 00:51:53.950
We would welcome the renegotiation of a treaty.

00:51:53.950 --> 00:51:56.750
I don't think we have-we have a formal request.

00:51:56.750 --> 00:51:58.109
We have not initiated a request.

00:51:58.109 --> 00:52:02.810
Now, the negotiation of those agreements,
which need to be passed by our legislative

00:52:02.810 --> 00:52:05.140
bodies, are not so quick.

00:52:05.140 --> 00:52:10.000
So I don't know if we should be waiting for
that to deal with this and other cases.

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:17.050
We certainly could move into that direction
for the future.

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:19.080
And I take the Fifth Amendment on your second
question.

00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:20.230
(Laughter, laughs.)

00:52:20.230 --> 00:52:22.840
CUNNINGHAM: Yes, here at the back table.

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:25.760
Wait for the microphone, sir.

00:52:25.760 --> 00:52:26.760
Thanks.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:30.369
Q: So, thank you very much, Madam Vice President,
for being here.

00:52:30.369 --> 00:52:35.070
And congratulations on the new government's
commitment to transparency and to development

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:36.500
issues.

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:38.960
So, my name's Itai Grinberg.

00:52:38.960 --> 00:52:40.160
I'm at Georgetown Law.

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:43.230
Important, I guess, also relevantly, I was
previously at the United States Office of

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:49.520
International Tax Counsel, where I worked
on offshore tax evasion issues.

00:52:49.520 --> 00:52:57.440
In prior governments, Panama developed a reputation
as being perhaps the most recalcitrant of

00:52:57.440 --> 00:53:03.240
the major or significant financial intermediary
jurisdictions around the world with respect

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:05.190
to offshore tax evasion issues.

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:11.380
And in this government, you've only recently
joined the Common Reporting Standard, which

00:53:11.380 --> 00:53:17.619
is kind of the new globally accepted standard
for what countries will do to try to address

00:53:17.619 --> 00:53:18.619
offshore tax evasion.

00:53:18.619 --> 00:53:21.580
I think you did that last week.

00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:26.350
Given this history, I think there's sort of
this reputational question that Panama faces.

00:53:26.350 --> 00:53:32.339
And the question is, just tangibly, are there
things Panama is prepared to do besides kind

00:53:32.339 --> 00:53:38.220
of the bare minimum to get off the, OK, they're
different than everyone else list that you're

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:40.930
prepared to share with this audience?

00:53:40.930 --> 00:53:46.700
So that would include, for example, changes
in rules about how lawyers and accountants

00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:49.260
do due diligence, rules about beneficial ownership.

00:53:49.260 --> 00:53:53.690
There are a variety of things that Panama
could do to change the way it's perceived.

00:53:53.690 --> 00:53:56.070
I'm curious if you have any thoughts about
that.

00:53:56.070 --> 00:53:57.930
SAINT MALO: Well, definitely.

00:53:57.930 --> 00:54:02.910
And actually, some of the things that you've
mentioned we've already done in this past

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:07.120
20 months in government.

00:54:07.120 --> 00:54:11.140
We have very strong know-your-client legislation
in Panama.

00:54:11.140 --> 00:54:15.860
Actually, I invite you to go to Panama and
try to open a bank account.

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:20.430
It's a lot harder than opening a bank account
in the United States.

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:23.690
We have know-your-client legislation.

00:54:23.690 --> 00:54:27.680
We have due diligence legislation.

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:38.339
The previous government approved a legislation
changing bearer shares and making it obligatory

00:54:38.339 --> 00:54:45.280
for lawyers to know the end owners of shares.

00:54:45.280 --> 00:54:52.060
And they passed that legislation and they
placed it in a hold for five years, until

00:54:52.060 --> 00:54:54.310
2018 or something like that.

00:54:54.310 --> 00:55:02.640
We reinstalled that legislation, which was
placed into effect January 1st this year.

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:07.970
So, today, important changes have been made
to our legislation.

00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:15.220
Now, there is a question that I don't think
is that well-known in terms of our commitment

00:55:15.220 --> 00:55:18.140
and in terms of our actions.

00:55:18.140 --> 00:55:23.990
And I think it's something that needs to be
put into the table and just figure out how

00:55:23.990 --> 00:55:24.990
we deal with it.

00:55:24.990 --> 00:55:28.520
And it's part of our reality.

00:55:28.520 --> 00:55:40.680
First of all, our taxing legislation, we do
not tax Panamanians for income generated outside

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:41.890
of Panama.

00:55:41.890 --> 00:55:45.710
That is our system, which works well for us.

00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:49.280
Now, we respect other countries' system(s).

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:57.250
And we are committed to helping other countries
follow the money of their own citizens that

00:55:57.250 --> 00:56:01.930
try to hide their money in other jurisdictions.

00:56:01.930 --> 00:56:06.900
We are committed to assisting those countries,
and we will continue to do everything that

00:56:06.900 --> 00:56:09.880
we can to assist those countries.

00:56:09.880 --> 00:56:17.530
However, the cost of implementing the Common
Reporting Standards is going to be very high

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:19.060
on our financial system.

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:20.900
And we're going to do it.

00:56:20.900 --> 00:56:30.860
But fear-I mean, nobody has put onto the table
what it represents for a small country to

00:56:30.860 --> 00:56:38.860
ensure that you commit to certain standards-because
other countries need that information; you

00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:39.860
don't.

00:56:39.860 --> 00:56:49.850
There is a large cost in terms of operations
for banks if they're going to exchange information.

00:56:49.850 --> 00:56:58.230
Our taxing authority can barely collect taxes
in Panama.

00:56:58.230 --> 00:57:06.560
I mean, we have our own problems of tax evasion
that we're working hard at conquering.

00:57:06.560 --> 00:57:20.010
Now, we don't only need to concentrate now
on strengthening our taxing authorities in

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:26.000
order for them to collect the taxes that we
need to improve the livelihoods of Panamanians,

00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:32.450
but now we need to make sure we strengthen
our tax authorities so that they can share

00:57:32.450 --> 00:57:37.390
information with the rest of the world, with
countries that are a lot more powerful and

00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:39.710
have a lot more resources than we do.

00:57:39.710 --> 00:57:41.410
Now, we're going to do it.

00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:44.840
We think it's fair that we do it.

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:49.339
But you need to know there is a cost involved
here.

00:57:49.339 --> 00:57:56.609
And that is something that I think needs to
be brought into the table at some point, because

00:57:56.609 --> 00:57:59.930
it's like-it's like developed countries-and
I understand the need for additional resources,

00:57:59.930 --> 00:58:06.619
and I understand the need to go after your
own citizens that find whatever way to evade

00:58:06.619 --> 00:58:08.300
taxes.

00:58:08.300 --> 00:58:16.260
And so international legislation has just
been getting stronger to move that fence,

00:58:16.260 --> 00:58:23.420
and in the process you're setting up some
standards to the rest of the world that are

00:58:23.420 --> 00:58:31.780
standards-that bear a high cost to your own
system at the-at the cost of your people,

00:58:31.780 --> 00:58:36.119
at the cost of money you need for development.

00:58:36.119 --> 00:58:43.470
So I just wish-since you're in that area,
you know, I just wish that that reality was

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:47.119
also placed on the table.

00:58:47.119 --> 00:58:56.070
Now, regarding are we willing to do something
more than just committing to the bare minimum,

00:58:56.070 --> 00:58:57.070
yes.

00:58:57.070 --> 00:59:02.619
I would love to see my country become a leader
in terms of transparency.

00:59:02.619 --> 00:59:06.800
And this is something that we've debated.

00:59:06.800 --> 00:59:17.140
Locally, we've installed an international
committee of experts as-last Friday, we installed

00:59:17.140 --> 00:59:24.839
it-as part of the measures that we've taken
to respond to the information that has come

00:59:24.839 --> 00:59:33.790
out through the Panama Papers and as a part
of our commitment to improve our system, committee

00:59:33.790 --> 00:59:38.260
to be co-chaired by Nobel Peace Prize Joseph
Stiglitz.

00:59:38.260 --> 00:59:45.310
Now, we all know where Professor Stiglitz's
views are in terms of transparency and in

00:59:45.310 --> 00:59:46.750
terms of development.

00:59:46.750 --> 00:59:54.339
And we are committed to just waiting for the
recommendations of that committee and see

00:59:54.339 --> 00:59:58.770
what information they provide there that will
help us be better.

00:59:58.770 --> 01:00:01.260
And, yes, I would love to be at the forefront.

01:00:01.260 --> 01:00:06.780
But you've got to understand, just committing
to what we've committed, it's not an easy

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:07.780
thing.

01:00:07.780 --> 01:00:13.349
We are having to face questions of whether
we devote money to education or we devote

01:00:13.349 --> 01:00:17.810
money to the standards.

01:00:17.810 --> 01:00:21.160
It's not an easy-it's not an easy position
to be in.

01:00:21.160 --> 01:00:25.310
But, yes, we're committing, and we are working
in that direction.

01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:28.099
CUNNINGHAM: Well, we've just crossed the 9:30
hour.

01:00:28.099 --> 01:00:33.840
And so, as I-as we bring matters to a close,
let me note that in this country we have a

01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:38.980
long tradition of vice presidents who use
the job as a platform for other jobs.

01:00:38.980 --> 01:00:39.980
(Laughter.)

01:00:39.980 --> 01:00:44.750
We have a more recent tradition of secretaries
of state, foreign ministers, even women secretaries

01:00:44.750 --> 01:00:48.200
of state using that as a position for further
jobs.

01:00:48.200 --> 01:00:51.430
And I commend that to Panama.

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:52.430
(Laughter.)

01:00:52.430 --> 01:00:53.430
Thank you all.

01:00:53.430 --> 01:00:54.880
Thank you all for-thank you for joining us
here.

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:55.880
Thank you all.

01:00:55.880 --> 01:00:57.990
Please join me in welcoming and thanking the
vice president.

01:00:57.990 --> 01:00:58.990
(Applause.)

01:00:58.990 --> 01:00:59.990
SAINT MALO: Thank you.

01:00:59.990 --> 01:01:00.990
Thank you very much.

01:01:00.990 --> 01:01:01.990
(Applause.)

01:01:01.990 --> 01:01:01.991
(END)

